    page and a full page load wipes it.
  - [1m[38;5;153msessionStorage[0m — survives refresh, dies when the tab closes, not shared across tabs. Readable by any script on the
    origin, so an XSS gets it.
  - [1m[38;5;153mlocalStorage[0m — survives browser restart. Same XSS exposure, longer window, and you now own an expiry/invalidation
    story — a stale identity sitting there after the server-side session died is a real failure mode.
  - [1m[38;5;153mhttpOnly[39m cookie set by the server[0m — JS can't read it, so it's the only option that resists XSS reading the identity;
    it's also the only one that needs a backend, which this repo doesn't have yet.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m, with the access wrapped in a small [38;5;153msession[39m module so no caller touches the storage
  API directly. Your app is multi-page ([38;5;153mindex.html[39m loads [38;5;153mapp.js[39m as a plain script), so in-memory loses the value on
  every navigation — that fails "work across the app." [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m buys browser-restart survival you didn't ask for and
  charges expiry complexity for it. The cookie option is genuinely the most secure and I'd pick it if a backend
  existed, but [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m is a stub and standing up a server is a much larger scope than what you described.

  One thing I want to be straight about: none of the client-side options make the identity [3mtrustworthy[0m. [38;5;153mlogin()[39m
  currently returns [38;5;153m{ success: true, user: username }[39m without checking anything, so whatever we store is self-asserted.
  That's fine for stamping forms for convenience; it is not an authorization mechanism, and I'll write that into the
  spec as an explicit non-goal rather than let it be assumed.
